# gh-393: Parity with dm.ai#707: Jira-style field names (key, labels, status, ...) in resolveAdoFieldName

## Description

Java fixed this in epam/dm.ai#707 (merged). Dart has the identical gap: `lib/src/integrations/ado/ado_work_item_ops.dart` `_fieldNames` mirrors Java's old `resolveFieldName` and has no Jira-style names, so an agent asking for `fields: ['key','labels']` sends them to ADO unchanged and gets `TF51535: Cannot find field key` (dmtools-agents#880 — the SM agent fails on its first ticket lookup).

**Add to `_fieldNames` (all lowercase keys, same as Java):**
`key→System.Id`, `status→System.State`, `labels→System.Tags`, `parent→System.Parent`, `updated→System.ChangedDate`, `created→System.CreatedDate`, `issuetype→System.WorkItemType`, `assignee→System.AssignedTo`, `reporter→System.CreatedBy`, `project→System.TeamProject`.

**Must stay as is:** names containing a `.` and unknown plain names pass through unchanged (custom fields like `Custom.Status` are never rewritten).

**Tests to mirror** (Java `AzureDevOpsClientTest`): each new name maps; case-insensitive (`KEY`, `Labels`); existing mappings and pass-through unchanged; a search whose request carries `fields=System.Id,System.Tags` and never `fields=key`.

**Verified live on an ADO test project (Java):** `fields=[key,labels,status,summary]` returns `System.Id / Tags / State / Title`.

